# ONE ROW PER LAYER PAIR A FILING HOLDS THAT ANOTHER DOCUMENT OBSERVED MOVING TOGETHER. The
# coupling is filed somewhere else; both of its ends are layers of this filing; and so this
# filing's remainders are not the independent quantizations its own arithmetic takes them for.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ A SPILLOVER IS UNINTENDED, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS NEITHER UNBALANCED NOR UNSETTLED BUT
#    BOTH. The layer at the far end is perfectly deliberate in its own filing. What nobody
#    meant is the reach into your remainder -- so neither set of books carries it, and neither
#    filer has anything to answer for. `merge-group-composition` watched delivery headcount
#    move the shift line; `merge-us-member` holds both layers and has not looked.
#
# ⛔⛔⛔ A REFERRAL, NEVER A VIOLATION. A filing is not the system, and a second document's
#    observation is a PROJECTION onto this one rather than an accusation against it. Nobody
#    here filed anything wrong: `enterprise-contract` states its coupling search as
#    `unmeasured` and its note says in prose exactly what this relation says in rows -- "the
#    same two layers are the subject of refutation.xml ... so this is the honest prior state
#    rather than a claim of independence". The author knew. No query found it.
#
# ⛔ THE FAR FILING IS NAMED AND THE BOUND IS NOT COPIED. merge-holding-composition argues at
#   length that a coupling carried up a level CANNOT survive at full strength and had to be
#   re-observed. A number copied across a boundary is a number nobody took.
-- pm:couplings/pm:coupling, projected onto every other filing holding both of its ends.
SELECT c.filing        AS observed_in,
       b.filing        AS borne_by,
       c.from_layer,
       c.to_layer,
       c.mode          AS observed_mode,
       c.unit          AS observed_unit,
       s.answer        AS their_search,
       sc.extent       AS their_extent
FROM      (
    :compose(entries/couplings.sqlc)
) c
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/every_layer.sqlc)
) b  ON b.layer = c.from_layer AND b.filing <> c.filing
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/every_layer.sqlc)
) b2 ON b2.filing = b.filing AND b2.layer = c.to_layer
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(epistemics/coupling_searches.sqlc)
) s  ON s.filing = b.filing
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(epistemics/scopes.sqlc)
) sc ON sc.filing = b.filing
